\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In contrast to the effective elastic or plastic properties obtained in classical micromechanics, it is presently not established in general that fracture in a heterogeneous material gives rise to a homogenized macroscopic material model with a well-defined fracture toughness obtained in an RVE limit.}}{2}{section*.5}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{The methodology is not used here as a proof of a homogenized fracture toughness in the mathematical sense. Rather, it is interpreted as a numerical counterpart of a fracture experiment. In this experiment-like setting, a representative material region containing microstructural features is subjected to so-called surfing boundary conditions that enforce complete separation, and the energetic driving force required for this process is recorded.}}{2}{section*.6}\protected@file@percent }
\@writefile{loc}{\contentsline {added}{Added: \truncate {\Changestruncatewidth }{In the present work, this requirement is not fully verified: only one prescribed macroscopic crack-growth direction and one initial crack location are considered, and no convergence study with respect to the size of the resolved porous region is performed.}}{2}{section*.7}\protected@file@percent }
